<ArticleBody depth={3}>

<p>When two requests change the same data at the same time, each can be correct on its own and the result can still be wrong. This article shows how to keep a business rule true under that kind of overlap, and when a request fails part-way through. It starts from the rule itself, then puts the whole change inside one transaction, and then picks a protection that fits the rule: a statement that checks and changes together, a constraint, a shared lock, or stronger isolation.</p>
<p>The short answer is that a transaction makes a group of changes commit or disappear together, but on its own it does not stop another session from acting on data you have just read. The protection has to cover the decision as well as the write. Often one statement can carry both, as in “take a place only if one is left”. A constraint can refuse a state outright. A rule that spans several rows needs every writer to meet at one shared lock, or the database’s strictest checking, which refuses one of two incompatible transactions so that it can be repeated. Each of these can end a transaction with an error, and the application must reconsider the whole operation, not just the statement that failed. A lost reply to <code>COMMIT</code> does not tell you whether the change committed.</p>
<p>The introduction raced two customers for the shop’s last mug in <a href={`${next}#a-transaction-protects-a-change-within-a-stated-contract`}>A transaction protects a change within a stated contract</a>. Here the same shop also runs classes, which give us a few more rules to protect. Mina Shah, Tariq Aziz and Edda Berg are customers. <code>classes.remaining</code> stores the places still free in each class, and each row in <code>reservations</code> is one booking, identified by the request that made it. Class 21, Tea tasting, has one place and no bookings. Class 22, Mug painting, has three places, and Tariq already holds one through request R7. Two instructors, Lena and Ravi, teach class 21.</p>

