<p>PostgreSQL 18 is the reference engine, and MySQL’s InnoDB and SQLite appear where their behaviour changes what you should write. The four numbered figures replay runs recorded on PostgreSQL 18.6: each step shows the statement, what the database returned, and the committed rows afterwards. The worksheet at the end runs them all again.</p>

<h2 id="start-with-the-fact-that-must-remain-true">Start with the fact that must remain true</h2>
<p>An <strong>invariant</strong> is a statement about the committed data that must hold after every commit. Before choosing any mechanism, write the invariants down. The class bookings have three:</p>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Places add up.</strong> For each class, <code>remaining</code> plus the number of its bookings equals its <code>capacity</code>. Taking a place and recording the booking always happen together.</li>
  <li><strong>One booking per customer per class.</strong> Mina can’t hold two places in class 22.</li>
  <li><strong>Class 21 keeps a teacher.</strong> At least one of its instructors is available.</li>
</ol>
<p>The rules have different shapes, and the shape decides which protections can work. The first ties a class row to a count of rows in another table, but every booking also changes that class row, so that row is where competing bookings meet. The second is about pairs of values in one table, which a unique constraint can state directly. The third is about a set of rows, and two changes can each touch a different row of the set without ever touching the same one.</p>
<p>Next, list the outcomes you would accept. Suppose Mina and Tariq both ask for class 21’s one place. Either Mina gets it and Tariq is told the class is full, or the other way round. Both booked is wrong, and so is neither booked with <code>remaining</code> at 0. That list is the test every later protection has to pass.</p>
<p>It also separates two kinds of fault. A booking that inserts a reservation but forgets to reduce <code>remaining</code> is wrong even when it runs alone, and an ordinary test finds it. A booking that reads <code>remaining</code>, checks it is above zero and then writes the new value is correct alone. It fails only when two run at the same moment, which is why single-user testing never shows it. Most of this article is about the second kind.</p>
<p>The schema already states the rules a database can check by itself. <code>CHECK (remaining BETWEEN 0 AND capacity)</code> stops a class going below zero or above capacity, <code>UNIQUE (class_id, owner_id)</code> is rule 2, and the primary key on <code>request_id</code> means the same request can be recorded only once.</p>
<Sql code={schemaSql} label="The class-booking tables, with the rules the database can check" />

<h3 id="put-the-complete-change-inside-a-boundary">Put the complete change inside a boundary</h3>
<p>A booking is two changes: take a place in <code>classes</code>, and record the booking in <code>reservations</code>. If the application sends them without <code>BEGIN</code>, PostgreSQL runs each statement as a transaction of its own and commits it as soon as it finishes. This is called <strong>autocommit</strong>, and it is what most drivers do unless asked otherwise.</p>
<p>Figure 1 follows Mina double-clicking Book in class 22, which sends two requests, R10 and R11, one after the other. Play it with <em>Each statement on its own</em>, then switch to <em>One transaction per booking</em> and watch the committed rows when R11’s insert is refused.</p>
<ScheduleFigure figure="clicks" number={1} caption="Recorded on PostgreSQL 18.6 in a single session. With separate statements, R11’s place stays taken after its insert is refused, so class 22 has 0 remaining and 2 bookings against a capacity of 3. Inside one transaction the refusal undoes R11’s place. The figure shows the clicks one after the other; when they arrive together, R11 waits on class 22’s row first and then fails in the same way, which the worksheet checks separately." />
<p>With separate statements, nothing undid R11’s first change, and a place disappeared. Inside a transaction, the refused insert left the whole transaction failed. PostgreSQL then refuses every further statement with error 25P02 until the transaction ends. (PostgreSQL labels each error with a five-character code: 23505 is a unique-constraint violation.) A <code>COMMIT</code> sent to a failed transaction is reported back as <code>ROLLBACK</code>. An application should read that result rather than assume the commit worked.</p>
<p>The three commands are short. <code>BEGIN</code> starts a transaction. <code>COMMIT</code> asks the database to make all its changes permanent and visible together. <code>ROLLBACK</code> discards them all. The guarantees are often summarised as ACID, and it helps to be precise about each letter:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Atomicity</strong> is what Figure 1 showed: all of a transaction’s changes commit, or none do.</li>
  <li><strong>Consistency</strong> means each commit takes the data from one state that meets the declared rules to another. The database can only enforce the rules it knows about. It knows the <code>CHECK</code> and the unique constraint; it does not know rule 1 or rule 3, which hold only if every transaction is written to keep them.</li>
  <li><strong>Isolation</strong> describes how much overlapping transactions can affect each other. The next sections are about it.</li>
  <li><strong>Durability</strong> means a committed change survives a crash, under the conditions the server is configured for. It is covered near the end.</li>
</ul>
<p>A <strong>savepoint</strong> marks a point inside a transaction that you can roll back to without abandoning the rest. It is not a commit: nothing inside it is permanent until the outer transaction commits. Savepoints also make it easy to undo too little. The worksheet set one after R11 took its place and before its insert, rolled back to the savepoint when the insert was refused, then committed. The place stayed taken with no booking, which is Figure 1’s broken state again.</p>
<p>Two other behaviours vary between engines. In PostgreSQL, a <code>CREATE TABLE</code> inside a transaction that rolls back leaves no table behind, while MySQL <a href={`${mysql}implicit-commit.html`}>commits implicitly</a> before and after most schema changes. And in PostgreSQL a sequence number taken by <code>nextval</code> is <a href={`${pg}functions-sequence.html`}>never handed back</a>, even if the transaction rolls back. The worksheet’s rolled-back transaction took value 1, and the next call returned 2. Gaps in generated IDs are normal.</p>
<p>The way an error affects the transaction also differs. In PostgreSQL any error fails the whole transaction. In InnoDB a duplicate-key error <a href={`${mysql}innodb-error-handling.html`}>rolls back only the statement</a> and leaves the transaction open, with R11’s place still taken. If the application then sends <code>COMMIT</code>, the place is lost exactly as in Figure 1’s first option. The safe habit on every engine is the same: after an error you didn’t plan to recover from, send <code>ROLLBACK</code>.</p>

<h3 id="let-the-database-reject-invalid-states-where-it-can">Let the database reject invalid states where it can</h3>
<p>A constraint is checked by the database for every writer, including administrative tools, imports and scripts that never pass through the booking code. Where a rule can be stated as a constraint, state it that way. Here the <code>CHECK</code> refuses a negative <code>remaining</code>, the unique constraint refuses Mina’s second booking in a class, the foreign keys refuse a booking for a class or customer that doesn’t exist, and the primary key refuses the same request twice.</p>
<p>A constraint also settles races between inserts. Checking “does Mina already have a booking here?” and then inserting can fail when two requests both check before either inserts: each sees no booking. A unique constraint does not have that gap. In the worksheet, R10 inserted Mina’s booking and had not yet committed when R11 tried to insert the same pair. R11 waited for R10’s transaction, and when R10 committed, R11 failed with 23505. Had R10 rolled back, R11’s insert would have gone ahead.</p>
<p>A constraint error is a domain answer, not a passing fault. “Mina already has a place in class 22” is true, and repeating the request will get the same answer, so report it. Later in the article we meet errors of a different kind, which mean only that the transaction collided with another one, and there repeating it is the right response.</p>
<p>Two of our three rules can’t be written as constraints. A PostgreSQL <code>CHECK</code> sees only the row being written, so it can’t count bookings in another table, and it can’t look at other instructors. <a href={`${next}constraints/#account-for-rules-the-schema-cannot-express`}>Accounting for rules the schema cannot express</a> covers the options in constraint design. What an insert should do when it meets existing data, such as <code>ON CONFLICT</code>, belongs to <a href={`${next}changing-data/#decide-what-should-happen-when-an-insert-meets-existing-data`}>Changing data with SQL</a>. For rules 1 and 3, the transactions themselves must keep the rule.</p>

<h2 id="two-correct-looking-transactions-can-make-an-incorrect-result">Two correct-looking transactions can make an incorrect result</h2>
<p>Mina, with request R8, and Tariq, with R9, both want class 21’s last place. The booking code reads <code>remaining</code>, and if it is above zero, works out the new value, writes it, and inserts the booking. Run alone, that code is correct.</p>
<p>Figure 2 draws the two requests as a <strong>history</strong>: one column, or lane, per session, with each statement in the order the database received it, and the moments where a statement waited. A useful way to judge a history is to compare it with the <strong>serial orders</strong>, where the same transactions run one at a time. Mina then Tariq: Tariq reads 0 and declines. Tariq then Mina: the same, the other way round. Both serial orders give an acceptable outcome. A concurrent history is safe if its result matches one of them.</p>
<p>Both requests run at <strong>Read Committed</strong>. How much one transaction sees of others’ work is set by its <strong>isolation level</strong>, and Read Committed is PostgreSQL’s default: each statement sees everything committed before that statement started. Before you play the first option, predict what class 21 will hold once both have committed. Both requests read 1 before either writes.</p>
<ScheduleFigure figure="race" number={2} caption="Recorded on PostgreSQL 18.6. The first three options run at Read Committed, PostgreSQL’s default; the last runs at Repeatable Read. Between a read and its write, the application works out the new value from what it read; that code isn’t shown. Each option replays its own recorded run, so the step where Tariq waits is real, not drawn in." />
<p>In the first option both transactions committed and class 21 ended with two bookings for one place: 0 remaining plus 2 booked against a capacity of 1. Tariq’s write replaced Mina’s with a value worked out from a read that was out of date by the time it was written. This is a <strong>lost update</strong>. No error was raised, because nothing in the database knew the value had come from an old read.</p>

<h3 id="the-protection-must-cover-the-decision-as-well-as-the-write">The protection must cover the decision as well as the write</h3>
<p>Both requests in Figure 2 already ran inside transactions. The transaction made each request’s two changes commit together, but it did nothing to the gap between reading <code>remaining</code> and writing it, and that gap is where Tariq read the same row.</p>
<p>The second option, <em>Subtract one, no condition</em>, lets the database do the arithmetic on whatever the row holds when the update runs. Tariq’s update waited for Mina’s transaction, then subtracted from her committed 0. The result, −1, broke the <code>CHECK</code>, so his statement failed with 23514. The rule held, but only because of the constraint, and Tariq’s application received an error rather than a clear answer. Without that <code>CHECK</code> the class would have been overbooked at −1.</p>
<p>The third option, <em>Change only if a place is left</em>, puts the decision and the change into one statement: <code>UPDATE … WHERE class_id = 21 AND remaining &gt; 0 RETURNING remaining</code>. At Read Committed, an update that reaches a row another transaction has changed waits for that transaction. If it commits, PostgreSQL <a href={`${pg}transaction-iso.html#XACT-READ-COMMITTED`}>checks the condition again</a> against the newly committed row. Tariq’s condition was then false, so no row changed and none came back. <code>RETURNING</code> is what lets the application tell the two answers apart: one row means a place was taken, no row means the class is full.</p>
<Prediction id="predict-rollback" title="What if Mina rolls back instead?" headingLevel={4}>
  <p>Use the third option. Mina’s conditional update has run and R8 is inserted, but not committed. Tariq’s identical update is waiting. Now Mina’s transaction rolls back instead of committing. What does Tariq’s statement return, and which bookings exist after he inserts R9 and commits?</p>
  <Fragment slot="answer">
    <p>Tariq gets the place. Once Mina’s transaction is gone, his update checks <code>remaining &gt; 0</code> against the row as it was before she started, which still says 1. It changes the row and returns 0. After his commit the bookings are R7 and R9, and class 21 has 0 remaining. The worksheet ran this schedule and got exactly that. A waiting statement does not decide anything until the transaction it waits for has ended, one way or the other.</p>
  </Fragment>
</Prediction>

<h3 id="a-repeatable-view-does-not-by-itself-prove-the-rule">A repeatable view does not by itself prove the rule</h3>
<p>The fourth option in Figure 2 keeps the read-then-write code and runs it at <strong>Repeatable Read</strong>. Each transaction then sees the data as it was at its first statement, a fixed view called a <strong>snapshot</strong>, for as long as it runs. When Tariq’s update reached a row that had changed since his snapshot, PostgreSQL refused it with 40001, “could not serialize access due to concurrent update”. His application then had to start again from <code>BEGIN</code>. The new attempt read 0 and declined.</p>
<p>That refusal is PostgreSQL’s contract, not a property of the name. InnoDB’s default level is also called Repeatable Read, but there an <code>UPDATE</code> <a href={`${mysql}innodb-consistent-read.html`}>acts on the latest committed row</a> rather than the snapshot. The same steps would let Tariq’s write succeed with no error and lose Mina’s update. That comes from MySQL’s documentation; the worksheet doesn’t run MySQL.</p>
<p>Even in PostgreSQL, Repeatable Read only notices conflicts between transactions that change the same row. Rule 3 doesn’t give it one. Lena and Ravi both ask to stop teaching class 21 at the same moment. Each request counts the available instructors, sees two, decides one may leave, and marks its own instructor unavailable. Play Figure 3 with <em>Repeatable Read</em> and watch the rule check at the end. Its other options are repairs that come later; two of them use Serializable, PostgreSQL’s strictest level.</p>
<ScheduleFigure figure="instructors" number={3} caption="Recorded on PostgreSQL 18.6. Only the instructors table is shown, because classes and reservations don’t change. The level each option uses is in its name; Lock class 21 first runs at Read Committed. In the two Serializable options, the second attempt after Ravi’s refused commit is a new transaction on the same connection." />
<p>Both transactions committed and class 21 has nobody. Neither changed a row the other changed, so there was no conflict for Repeatable Read to refuse. In either serial order, the second request would have counted one and declined. Two decisions made from the same rows, each changing a different row, is called <strong>write skew</strong>. A stable view keeps each transaction’s reads consistent, but it does not stop two consistent reads leading to incompatible decisions. PostgreSQL’s Repeatable Read is <em>snapshot isolation</em>, and write skew is the outcome snapshot isolation allows.</p>

<h2 id="choose-protection-for-the-invariant-you-actually-have">Choose protection for the invariant you actually have</h2>
<p>The figures so far point to a short rule of thumb. Match the protection to the shape of the rule:</p>
<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Choosing a protection from the shape of the rule"><table>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Shape of the rule</th><th scope="col">Here</th><th scope="col">Protection that fits</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>One row changes relative to its own current value</td><td>Take a place if one is left</td><td>A conditional <code>UPDATE</code> with <code>RETURNING</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>A value must stay within limits in its own row</td><td><code>remaining</code> between 0 and capacity</td><td>A <code>CHECK</code> constraint</td></tr>
    <tr><td>A combination of values must not repeat</td><td>One booking per customer per class</td><td>A <code>UNIQUE</code> constraint</td></tr>
    <tr><td>A decision depends on several rows, or on a count</td><td>Class 21 keeps an instructor</td><td>A shared row that every writer locks before reading, or the Serializable level with whole-transaction retry, or a design that moves the rule into one row (all three below)</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table></div>

<h3 id="express-a-change-and-its-condition-together">Express a change and its condition together</h3>
<p>Here is the complete booking, using the conditional update from Figure 2. The place is taken first, so if the request has to wait, it waits before it has inserted anything.</p>
<Sql code={booking} label="A booking that decides and changes in one statement" />
<p>The application reads the outcome of each statement. One row from the update means Mina has the place, and no row means the class is full. If the class might not exist, that also returns no row, so check separately if the difference matters. A 23505 from the insert means she already has a place.</p>
<p>The listing relies on three assumptions. The class is identified by its primary key, so the statement can only ever touch one row. The update changes that row relative to its current value. And it runs at Read Committed in PostgreSQL, which checks the condition again after a wait. At Repeatable Read the same statement fails with 40001 instead, and other engines have their own rules, so don’t carry the listing across without checking.</p>
<p>A conditional update decides from the committed row; it does not wait for changes it has no reason to. In the worksheet, a transaction freeing a place in a full class 21 was still open when Tariq’s update ran. His update saw the committed value 0, matched nothing, and returned at once without waiting. That is a correct answer, since a serial order with Tariq first gives the same result, but it means “full” can be true for only a moment.</p>
<p>Sometimes the decision needs code the database can’t run, such as a price calculation. Then a <strong>version check</strong> does the same job. Read the value, decide, and make the write conditional on the value still being the one you read. In the worksheet, both sessions read 1. Tariq’s guarded update waited for Mina, and after she committed it changed no rows. Many designs keep a <code>version</code> column that every change increments, which makes the check independent of the business columns.</p>
<Sql code={versionCheck} label="Write only if the value read earlier is unchanged" />
<p>A failed version check means the decision was made from out-of-date data. The answer is to read again and decide again. Sending the same write a second time would just repeat the out-of-date decision.</p>

<h3 id="coordinate-decisions-that-span-several-rows">Coordinate decisions that span several rows</h3>
<p>Rule 3 depends on two rows, and a conditional update only checks the row it changes. One repair is to make every change to class 21’s instructors meet at a single row first. Switch Figure 3 to <em>Lock class 21 first</em>. Each request starts with <code>SELECT … FOR UPDATE</code> on class 21’s row in <code>classes</code>, which <strong>locks</strong> the row: another transaction that tries to lock or change it waits until this one ends. Ravi’s request waits for that lock before it has counted anything. When it gets the lock, its count is a new statement, so at Read Committed it sees Lena’s commit and finds one instructor. It declines.</p>
<Sql code={leaveWithLock} label="Lena’s request, coordinating on class 21’s row" />
<p>The class row works as a <strong>guard row</strong> because it is stable: it exists whether or not anyone teaches the class, and every writer can find it. A <code>FOR UPDATE</code> lock lasts until the transaction ends, so it covers the count, the decision and the change. (Rolling back to a savepoint taken before the lock also releases it.) The protection only works if every path that can make an instructor unavailable takes part. An administrative screen or a nightly import that updates <code>instructors</code> without locking the class first can break the rule again, because the database only makes participants wait for each other.</p>
<p>Locking the rows you counted is not the same thing. Take a stated variation of the example in which class 22 has two places, Tariq holds one, and bookings are counted rather than stored in <code>remaining</code>. Mina, as R10, and Edda, as R11, each lock class 22’s reservations with <code>FOR UPDATE</code> and count them. In the worksheet, Edda waited on R7’s lock. After Mina inserted R10 and committed, Edda’s statement still returned only R7, because the new row was not among the rows her statement had found. She inserted R11, and class 22 ended with three bookings for two places. Row locks protect rows that exist. They do not stop new ones appearing. PostgreSQL also refuses <code>FOR UPDATE</code> on a query with <code>count(*)</code>, because an aggregate doesn’t return the rows it counted.</p>
<p>The other approach is to move the rule into one row. That is what <code>classes.remaining</code> does for rule 1: instead of counting bookings, each booking changes one counter, and a conditional update on one row is enough. Rule 3 could have the same treatment with an available-instructor count on the class row and a <code>CHECK</code> that it stays at 1 or more. The cost is that every writer must keep the counter in step with the rows it summarises.</p>
<p>The third approach is to let the database detect the problem, by running every one of these transactions at Serializable. That comes next. <a href={`${next}locking/#give-the-invariant-a-stable-coordination-row`}>Designing changes that coordinate with locks</a> works through complete guard-row protocols, including moves, deletions and the choice of lock mode, for the version of this example that counts bookings.</p>

<h2 id="understand-the-isolation-contract-behind-the-choice">Understand the isolation contract behind the choice</h2>
<p>An <strong>isolation level</strong> is the engine’s promise about which concurrent histories it allows. The SQL standard defines four levels by the anomalies each must prevent. Berenson and colleagues showed in 1995 that those definitions are ambiguous and miss lost updates and write skew, and engines have filled the gaps in different ways. So treat a level name as the name of one engine’s contract, and read that contract.</p>
<p>PostgreSQL has three distinct levels; asking for Read Uncommitted gives Read Committed. Here is what each allows, using the outcomes from this article:</p>
<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Which anomalies each PostgreSQL 18 isolation level allows"><table class="anomalies">
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Anomaly</th><th scope="col">What happens</th><th scope="col">Read Committed</th><th scope="col">Repeatable Read</th><th scope="col">Serializable</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>{anomalies.map(([name, what, rc, rr, ser]) => <tr><th scope="row">{name}</th><td>{what}</td><td>{rc}</td><td>{rr}</td><td>{ser}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>“Yes” means the anomaly can happen. “Refused” means PostgreSQL stops it by failing one of the transactions, which the application must then handle.</p>
<p>A set of transactions is <strong>serialisable</strong> if the result of running them together is the same as the result of running the committed ones one at a time, in some order. It does not mean the transactions didn’t overlap, and the order doesn’t have to match the order in which they committed. <a href={`${next}isolation/#a-serial-order-does-not-establish-a-real-time-order`}>Reading and testing isolation guarantees</a> shows why that last point matters to a client that saw one commit before another.</p>

<h3 id="versions-and-locks-answer-different-questions">Versions and locks answer different questions</h3>
<p>PostgreSQL keeps more than one version of a row. An update writes a new version and leaves the old one for any transaction whose snapshot was taken before the update committed. This is <strong>multiversion concurrency control</strong>, or MVCC. Readers pick the version their snapshot allows, so a plain read never waits for a writer. Writers to the same row still conflict. A transaction that changes a row holds a lock on it until it ends, and another transaction that wants to change or lock that row waits.</p>
<p>A Read Committed transaction takes a new snapshot for each statement. Repeatable Read and Serializable take one at the first statement and keep it. Every level sees the transaction’s own uncommitted changes. The worksheet checked what one statement from Tariq does while Mina’s conditional update of class 21 is uncommitted, and after she commits:</p>
<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="What Tariq’s statement does while Mina’s change is uncommitted and after she commits"><table>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Tariq’s statement</th><th scope="col">While Mina’s change is uncommitted</th><th scope="col">After she commits, at Read Committed</th><th scope="col">After she commits, at Repeatable Read</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>{claims.visibility.map(row => <tr><th scope="row"><code>{row.statement}</code></th><td>{row.during}</td><td>{row.readCommitted}</td><td>{row.repeatableRead}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>So a snapshot decides what a read can see, and a lock decides who has to wait. A snapshot does not reserve anything: holding an old view of class 21 doesn’t stop anyone else booking it.</p>
<p>Statement snapshots also matter to reports. In the worksheet, a report at Read Committed read class 21’s <code>remaining</code> as 1. Mina’s booking then committed, and the report’s next statement counted 1 booking. Added together, that looks like 2 against a capacity of 1, although no committed state was ever wrong: the two numbers came from different moments. Run at Repeatable Read, the same report read 1 and 0, which agree. A report built from several statements needs one snapshot.</p>
<p>Old versions are not free. They take space until no snapshot needs them, and cleaning them up is ongoing work. <a href={`${next}physical-storage/#updates-leave-work-behind-them`}>How stored rows become physical work</a> explains what that costs.</p>

<h3 id="stronger-isolation-can-reject-a-result-instead-of-waiting">Stronger isolation can reject a result instead of waiting</h3>
<p>PostgreSQL’s Serializable level uses <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1208.4179">serializable snapshot isolation</a>. Each transaction runs on a snapshot as it would at Repeatable Read, and the database also records what each one read. Those records don’t make anyone wait. When it finds overlapping transactions whose reads and writes could not have happened in any serial order, it fails one of them with 40001, at a statement or at <code>COMMIT</code>.</p>
<p>Switch Figure 3 to <em>Serializable, repeat the transaction</em>. Nothing waits or fails until Ravi’s commit, which is refused. His application then starts the whole transaction again, counts one instructor, and declines. The rule holds without a lock, and without anyone deciding which row to lock.</p>
<p>That convenience has three conditions. Every transaction that can affect the rule must run at Serializable: one Read Committed writer can break it unseen. The application must be ready to repeat any transaction, sometimes when no real conflict happened, because the tracking is conservative. And until a Serializable transaction commits, its reads <a href={`${pg}transaction-iso.html#XACT-SERIALIZABLE`}>may not be valid</a>, so don’t act on them outside the database before the commit succeeds.</p>
<p>Other engines reach for different mechanisms at the same names, and the difference changes what code you write. SQLite appears in WAL mode, its write-ahead log setting, which lets readers carry on while one connection writes. A plain <code>BEGIN</code> in SQLite is deferred: it takes no lock until the transaction first reads or writes. Each card says where its evidence comes from.</p>
<div class="contract-cards" role="list" aria-label="Isolation contracts by engine and setting">
  {contracts.map(c => <div class="contract-card" role="listitem">
    <p class="contract-name"><strong>{c.engine}</strong> · {c.setting}</p>
    <dl>
      <dt>What a read sees</dt><dd>{c.reads}</dd>
      <dt>When writes conflict</dt><dd>{c.conflict}</dd>
      <dt>What the application does</dt><dd>{c.application}</dd>
    </dl>
    <p class="contract-evidence">{c.evidence}</p>
  </div>)}
</div>

<h2 id="an-aborted-transaction-must-be-reconsidered-as-a-whole">An aborted transaction must be reconsidered as a whole</h2>
<p>Every protection in this article can stop a transaction. That is how it keeps the rule: preventing a bad commit sometimes means refusing good work. Each kind of stop means something different, so the application has to tell them apart. These are PostgreSQL’s codes; other engines have their own.</p>
<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="How a transaction can end, and what the application does"><table>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Outcome</th><th scope="col">What happened</th><th scope="col">What the application does</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>{outcomes.map(([what, meaning, action]) => <tr><th scope="row">{what}</th><td>{meaning}</td><td>{action}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>How much an error undoes depends on the engine. In PostgreSQL any error fails the whole transaction. In InnoDB a deadlock rolls back the whole transaction, but a lock-wait timeout or a duplicate key rolls back only the statement, unless <code>innodb_rollback_on_timeout</code> is set. Sending <code>ROLLBACK</code> after an unexpected error gives the same starting point everywhere.</p>
<p>A <strong>deadlock</strong> needs two transactions that each hold a lock the other wants. Suppose Mina’s R8 holds class 21’s place. Mina moves R8 from class 21 to 22, while Tariq moves R7 the other way. If each move locks the class it is leaving first, Mina holds 21 and asks for 22 while Tariq holds 22 and asks for 21. Neither can continue. PostgreSQL checks for this after a wait of <code>deadlock_timeout</code>, one second by default, and aborts one of them with 40P01. In the recorded run it chose Mina, but which one it picks <a href={`${pg}explicit-locking.html#LOCKING-DEADLOCKS`}>can’t be predicted</a>.</p>
<p>When both moves lock the classes in ascending <code>class_id</code> order, the cycle can’t form. Tariq waited for class 21 before he held anything, Mina finished, and then Tariq’s move went through. Both class rules held at the end.</p>
<Sql code={move} label="Moving R8 to class 22, with both class rows locked in a fixed order" />
<p>A real move also checks, after taking the locks, that R8 is still where the request expected it. A fixed order removes this cycle, not every deadlock, because foreign keys, unique indexes and other writers can add their own lock dependencies. <a href={`${next}locking/#acquire-several-resources-in-a-consistent-order`}>Acquiring several resources in a consistent order</a> develops the move in full.</p>

<h3 id="retrying-one-statement-can-preserve-a-stale-decision">Retrying one statement can preserve a stale decision</h3>
<p>Switch Figure 3 to <em>Serializable, repeat the failed statement</em>. Ravi’s commit is refused as before, but this time the application repeats only the statement that failed, inside a new transaction. Nothing conflicts now, so it commits, and class 21 has nobody. The retry carried a decision made from the old count of two into a transaction that never counted.</p>
<p>A retry means running the whole operation again from <code>BEGIN</code>, including its reads and the code that decides from them. In practice the retry loop wraps the application function that reads and decides, not a list of SQL statements that a driver can replay. Retry only on errors that mean “try again”, such as 40001 and 40P01. Give up after a few attempts, waiting a short, slightly random time between them so that the same transactions don’t collide again, and report the failure. Nothing outside the database may happen inside the loop, or each attempt will repeat it.</p>
<p>Keep transactions short. Prepare input before <code>BEGIN</code>, and never wait for a person or another service while a transaction is open. A lock held during a network call makes every later booking for that class wait, and at Serializable a longer transaction is more likely to be refused.</p>
<p>Sometimes waiting is the wrong answer. <code>FOR UPDATE NOWAIT</code> fails at once with 55P03 if the row is locked, and <code>FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED</code> leaves locked rows out of the result. Neither means “full”; they mean the decision wasn’t made. <a href={`${next}locking/#decide-what-waiting-and-skipping-mean-for-the-application`}>Deciding what waiting and skipping mean</a> covers when each fits.</p>
<p>Every booking for class 21 changes the same row, so bookings for a popular class take turns, however idle the rest of the database is. <a href={`${next}concurrent-load/#a-hot-row-can-limit-a-mostly-idle-database`}>A hot row can limit a mostly idle database</a> shows how to recognise that. Retry policy across a whole request, including timeouts and limits, is in <a href={`${next}request-recovery/#repeat-the-complete-logical-transaction-when-required`}>Recovering database requests safely</a>.</p>

<h3 id="losing-the-reply-does-not-prove-rollback">Losing the reply does not prove rollback</h3>
<p>The booking service sends <code>COMMIT</code> for Mina’s R8, and the connection fails before the reply arrives. The service can’t tell whether the database received the <code>COMMIT</code>. Play Figure 4 with <em>Committed; service books again</em>, then try the other two options.</p>
<ScheduleFigure figure="reply" number={4} caption="Recorded on PostgreSQL 18.6 at Read Committed. The broken connection is staged. In the two committed options, the worksheet sent COMMIT and closed the connection without reading the reply. In the third, it ended the server process for that session before sending COMMIT. A real failure can happen at either point, and the service sees the same thing both times." />
<p>Treating the failure as a rollback told Mina the class was full while the database held her booking. The repair is to give each operation an identity that is stored with its result, here the <code>request_id</code>, and to look it up before doing anything else. If R8 exists, the first attempt committed. If not, repeating the whole booking is safe. The primary key on <code>request_id</code> adds a second guard: a repeated insert of R8 fails with 23505 rather than booking twice.</p>
<p>That lookup is the database half of the answer. Choosing and passing operation identities through a service, and resolving outcomes the caller never received, are covered in <a href={`${next}request-recovery/#resolve-an-outcome-that-the-caller-did-not-receive`}>Resolving an outcome the caller did not receive</a> and <a href={`${next}request-recovery/#make-the-operation-identity-part-of-the-data-contract`}>Making the operation identity part of the data contract</a>.</p>

<h2 id="commit-has-a-durability-boundary-and-a-system-boundary">Commit has a durability boundary and a system boundary</h2>
<p>With PostgreSQL’s default <code>synchronous_commit = on</code>, a successful reply to <code>COMMIT</code> means the transaction’s record has reached durable storage in the write-ahead log, the file PostgreSQL records every change in before it changes the table files. The commit therefore survives a crash of the server. Turning <a href={`${pg}wal-async-commit.html`}>synchronous commit off</a> lets the reply come before that flush. A crash can then lose the most recent commits, up to about three times <code>wal_writer_delay</code> (600 ms with the default 200 ms setting), but the database stays consistent. That is different from turning <code>fsync</code> off, which can corrupt it. The setting can be changed for one transaction, so a low-value change can trade durability for speed while bookings keep the default. The worksheet’s server ran with both <code>synchronous_commit</code> and <code>fsync</code> on.</p>
<p>That promise covers one server and its storage. Whether a replica has the change when a client is told it committed depends on the replication settings; <a href={`${next}replicas-and-partitions/#decide-what-an-acknowledgement-promises`}>Deciding what an acknowledgement promises</a> covers that. Losing the server, or deleting a booking by mistake, needs another copy, which is the subject of <a href={`${next}backup-and-restore/`}>Backing up and restoring relational data</a>.</p>

<h3 id="a-database-rollback-cannot-undo-an-external-effect">A database rollback cannot undo an external effect</h3>
<p>Suppose a booking also sends Mina a confirmation email. The database can roll back its own changes, but it can’t unsend an email, so where the email is sent decides what can go wrong:</p>
<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Where to send a confirmation email, and what can go wrong"><table>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Where the email is sent</th><th scope="col">What can go wrong</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><th scope="row">Inside the transaction, before <code>COMMIT</code></th><td>The commit can fail or be refused, so Mina is told about a booking that doesn’t exist. The transaction also holds class 21’s lock while it waits for the mail server.</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">After <code>COMMIT</code> returns</th><td>The process can stop between the commit and the email, so Mina is booked and never told. If the reply to <code>COMMIT</code> is lost, the service may not send it at all.</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">From an outbox row written in the same transaction</th><td>The booking and the intention to send commit together. A separate worker sends the email and marks the row sent. If the worker stops after sending, the email can go twice, so the receiver should recognise R8.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table></div>
<p>The outbox works because it makes the intention part of the data the transaction protects. <a href={`${next}background-work/#commit-the-business-change-and-the-intent-together`}>Committing the business change and the intent together</a> develops it, and <a href={`${next}connections/`}>Managing connections and transaction lifetimes</a> covers how long a transaction and its connection should live.</p>

<h2 id="check-the-argument-with-competing-sessions-and-failures">Check the argument with competing sessions and failures</h2>
<p>A protection is an argument: this rule, these writers, these reads, this mechanism, so the bad outcomes can’t commit. Test the argument with the histories that would break it. Open two connections and send their statements in the order a history gives, so the interleaving is chosen, not left to chance. Use a third connection to confirm that a statement is really waiting: PostgreSQL’s <code>pg_stat_activity</code> view shows <code>wait_event_type = 'Lock'</code> for it. A pause in your script proves nothing. Choose data that would show the failure: class 21 has one place precisely so that an overbooking is visible.</p>
<p>Random load is weaker evidence than it looks. The worksheet raced 20 clients, customers 101 to 120 used only for this test, for class 21’s one place, 20 rounds for each protocol. The read-then-write code booked all 20 clients in every round. The conditional update booked exactly one in every round. A second test sent two clients at once with no attempt to line them up, 100 times, and the read-then-write code overbooked all 100 times, because on this machine both reads always arrived before either write. On another machine, or across a network, the same code might pass a long load test. Load tests tell you about timing on one system. Chosen histories test the protocol.</p>
<p><a href={verifyUrl} download="verify.mjs">Download the worksheet</a> and <a href={modelUrl} download="model.mjs">its schedules</a>, and keep the two files side by side. It runs every option of every figure, checking each statement’s result and the committed rows after each step, along with the other checks described in this article: the waiting and visibility table, the rollback prediction, the concurrent clicks, the failed transaction, the savepoint, schema changes and sequences, the counting variation, both moves, the version check, the report, and the load tests. It needs Node 22 or later and a disposable PostgreSQL 18 server that accepts connections without a password. Run <code>node verify.mjs --host 127.0.0.1 --port 57612</code>. It creates its own database, refuses one that already exists and drops it at the end.</p>
<p>The recorded run used PostgreSQL 18.6 on macOS (Apple silicon), with Read Committed as the default level, <code>deadlock_timeout</code> of one second, no lock or statement timeout, and <code>synchronous_commit</code> and <code>fsync</code> on. All 235 checks passed. <a href={sqliteUrl} download="verify-sqlite.py">A second script</a> checks the SQLite cards with Python’s standard library: <code>python3 verify-sqlite.py</code>. It passed on SQLite 3.54.0. The MySQL entries come from its documentation and were not run.</p>
<p>Before relying on a protocol, write down:</p>
<ol class="checklist">
  <li>The rule, as a statement about committed rows.</li>
  <li>Every path that can write data the rule depends on, including imports, tools and background jobs.</li>
  <li>What each path reads, and what it assumes stays true until it commits.</li>
  <li>The protection, and why it covers that assumption for every path.</li>
  <li>What the application does with each way the transaction can end, including a lost reply.</li>
  <li>The histories that would break the rule if the protection were missing, run as tests.</li>
</ol>
<p>Run those tests again whenever a writer, a query, the isolation level or the engine changes.</p>

<h2 id="sources">Sources and further reading</h2>
<p>Checked on 21 October 2026. The people, classes and requests are illustrative. The figure schedules and worksheet checks ran on PostgreSQL 18.6 and SQLite 3.54.0; the MySQL behaviour is taken from its documentation and was not run.</p>
<ul>
  <li>PostgreSQL 18: <a href={`${pg}transaction-iso.html`}>Transaction isolation</a>, for the three levels, rechecking after a wait, the 40001 failures and serializable reads; <a href={`${pg}mvcc-serialization-failure-handling.html`}>Serialization failure handling</a>, for repeating whole transactions; <a href={`${pg}explicit-locking.html`}>Explicit locking</a>, for row locks, deadlocks and savepoints; <a href={`${pg}sql-select.html#SQL-FOR-UPDATE-SHARE`}>The locking clause</a>, for <code>NOWAIT</code>, <code>SKIP LOCKED</code> and the aggregate restriction; <a href={`${pg}ddl-constraints.html`}>Constraints</a>; <a href={`${pg}functions-sequence.html`}>Sequence functions</a>; <a href={`${pg}wal-async-commit.html`}>Asynchronous commit</a>; <a href={`${pg}errcodes-appendix.html`}>Error codes</a>.</li>
  <li>MySQL 8.4: <a href={`${mysql}innodb-transaction-isolation-levels.html`}>InnoDB isolation levels</a>, <a href={`${mysql}innodb-consistent-read.html`}>Consistent nonlocking reads</a>, <a href={`${mysql}innodb-locking-reads.html`}>Locking reads</a>, <a href={`${mysql}innodb-next-key-locking.html`}>Phantom rows</a>, <a href={`${mysql}innodb-error-handling.html`}>InnoDB error handling</a> and <a href={`${mysql}implicit-commit.html`}>Statements that cause an implicit commit</a>.</li>
  <li>SQLite: <a href={`${sqlite}isolation.html`}>Isolation in SQLite</a>, <a href={`${sqlite}lang_transaction.html`}>Transactions</a>, <a href={`${sqlite}wal.html`}>Write-ahead logging</a> and <a href={`${sqlite}rescode.html#busy_snapshot`}>SQLITE_BUSY_SNAPSHOT</a>.</li>
  <li>Hal Berenson, Phil Bernstein, Jim Gray, Jim Melton, Elizabeth O’Neil and Patrick O’Neil, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/cs/0701157">A critique of ANSI SQL isolation levels</a> (SIGMOD 1995): why the standard’s anomaly definitions miss lost updates and write skew, and the definition of snapshot isolation.</li>
  <li>Dan Ports and Kevin Grittner, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1208.4179">Serializable snapshot isolation in PostgreSQL</a> (VLDB 2012): how PostgreSQL’s Serializable level detects dangerous dependencies.</li>
  <li>Continue in this collection with <a href={`${next}locking/`}>Designing changes that coordinate with locks</a>, for complete guard-row protocols, and <a href={`${next}isolation/`}>Reading and testing isolation guarantees</a>, for engine contracts and how to test them. <a href={next}>Relational databases</a> is the introduction this article builds on.</li>
</ul>

